  "prediction": "**Pacific Crest Trail Overcrowding and Safety Concerns**\n\nThe Pacific Crest Trail (PCT) has seen a significant increase in hikers in recent years, particularly since the release of Cheryl Strayed's memoir \"Wild\" and the subsequent movie adaptation. According to the Pacific Crest Trail Association (PCTA), more than 4,000 long-distance hiking permits have been issued in 2016, with over 3,000 hikers attempting the full 2,650-mile trail.\n\nWhile the PCT is open to all who wish to hike it, the influx of hikers has raised concerns about overcrowding, environmental damage, and safety. The PCTA has implemented a permit system with a daily quota of 50 hikers to mitigate these issues, but the system is still struggling to keep up with demand.\n\nThe desert environments along the first 700 miles of the trail are particularly vulnerable to damage, and the PCTA is working to prevent the establishment of shelters and other infrastructure that could exacerbate the problem. The U.S. Forest Service has also reported a significant increase in helicopter rescues and searches, with 20 rescues already occurring in the first 50 miles of the trail by April 26.\n\nDespite the challenges, many hikers are drawn to the PCT for its transformative power and natural beauty. Cheryl Strayed's story, in particular, has inspired a new generation of hikers, with anecdotal evidence suggesting that more women are taking on the challenge.\n\nHowever, the PCTA is urging hikers to be mindful of their impact on the trail and the environment. The organization has launched a campaign called #responsiblywild, which promotes safety and \"leave no trace\" practices, such as protecting water quality, burying human waste, and packing out trash.\n\nAs the PCT continues to attract more hikers, it is essential that the trail community comes together to address the challenges of overcrowding and environmental damage. By working together, hikers can ensure that the PCT remains a wild and beautiful experience for generations to come.\n\n**Key Statistics:**\n\n* Over 4,000 long-distance hiking permits issued in 2016\n* Over 3,000 hikers attempting the full 2,650-mile trail\n* 50 hikers per day quota implemented by the PCTA\n* 20 rescues already occurring in the first 50 miles of the trail by April 26\n* 30% increase in female hikers on the trail\n* Average cost of a through-hike",
